1
00:00:36,266 --> 00:00:39,060
WE ARE ALL TRYING HERE

2
00:00:48,736 --> 00:00:50,363
<i>I should've fallen in love.</i>

3
00:00:51,447 --> 00:00:53,741
<i>Instead of struggling</i>
<i>to make words come together,</i>

4
00:00:54,492 --> 00:00:55,785
<i>I should've fallen in love.</i>

5
00:00:57,620 --> 00:00:59,330
<i>The words I once forced together</i>

6
00:00:59,831 --> 00:01:02,625
<i>are now pouring out faster</i>
<i>than I can contain them.</i>

7
00:01:04,043 --> 00:01:07,046
<i>It feels like an engine inside me</i>
<i>has just started.</i>

8
00:01:07,130 --> 00:01:08,006
<i>Vroom.</i>

9
00:01:09,883 --> 00:01:11,759
<i>It feels like everything</i>

10
00:01:12,260 --> 00:01:13,845
<i>is working out on its own.</i>

11
00:01:13,928 --> 00:01:14,888
<i>Vroom.</i>

12
00:01:15,638 --> 00:01:18,183
<i>Who knew I had</i>
<i>such a powerful engine inside me?</i>

13
00:01:19,184 --> 00:01:20,727
<i>I should've fallen in love.</i>

14
00:01:22,187 --> 00:01:24,022
<i>Why didn't you tell me you've got a girl?</i>

15
00:01:30,778 --> 00:01:31,946
Hey, I think it's true.

16
00:01:32,947 --> 00:01:35,742
<i>Not long ago, someone threw a rock</i>
<i>through the window at Agit.</i>

17
00:01:36,326 --> 00:01:37,869
Apparently, Eun-a was behind it.

18
00:01:37,952 --> 00:01:38,912
Why would she do that?

19
00:01:38,995 --> 00:01:41,372
Remember when Gyeong-se
posted crap about you in the group chat

20
00:01:41,456 --> 00:01:42,540
and you two were fighting?

21
00:01:42,624 --> 00:01:44,208
Apparently, that's when she did it.

22
00:01:50,089 --> 00:01:51,507
Isn't Eun-a so cool?

23
00:02:26,501 --> 00:02:27,585
Holy shit!

24
00:02:30,964 --> 00:02:32,799
When are you going to get married?

25
00:02:34,801 --> 00:02:38,680
<i>The only girls in my life were those</i>
<i>who dreaded sitting next to me in class</i>

26
00:02:39,597 --> 00:02:41,307
<i>until a woman</i>
<i>as shiny as a blade walked in.</i>

27
00:02:42,850 --> 00:02:44,727
<i>There's nothing I wouldn't do now.</i>

28
00:02:45,436 --> 00:02:46,771
<i>What would you like me to do?</i>

29
00:02:47,814 --> 00:02:49,440
<i>Who should I kill for you?</i>

30
00:02:51,484 --> 00:02:53,486
I really don't understand you.

31
00:02:53,569 --> 00:02:55,863
How could you like a guy
like Hwang Dong-man?

32
00:02:56,447 --> 00:02:58,491
Don't try to understand.
You don't have to.

33
00:02:58,574 --> 00:03:00,284
Why do you keep trying to understand it?

34
00:03:00,368 --> 00:03:01,285
Are you a clarity freak?

35
00:03:01,369 --> 00:03:04,580
When I say I don't understand,
it means I don't like it.

36
00:03:04,664 --> 00:03:05,748
That's what I'm saying.

37
00:03:06,541 --> 00:03:09,961
Why don't you just say you don't like it,
instead of saying you don't understand?

38
00:03:10,586 --> 00:03:11,796
Acting like you're nice.

39
00:03:12,588 --> 00:03:15,216
I'd rather you be honest
and straightforward.

40
00:03:17,427 --> 00:03:18,553
You keep crossing the line.

41
00:03:19,178 --> 00:03:20,805
You crossed it first.

42
00:03:21,556 --> 00:03:25,351
You know people are fragile,
and that a single glance or word

43
00:03:25,435 --> 00:03:28,062
can send them to heaven or hell.

44
00:03:28,771 --> 00:03:30,064
But you still mock me

45
00:03:30,898 --> 00:03:31,733
and sigh at me.

46
00:03:35,737 --> 00:03:36,779
I won't stand for it.

47
00:03:39,699 --> 00:03:40,533
Hey.

48
00:03:43,119 --> 00:03:45,038
As you can see, I've got thick hair.

49
00:03:45,121 --> 00:03:48,458
If you want to grab my hair and fight me,
you'd better be ready to lose a finger.

50
00:03:54,672 --> 00:03:55,715
-Goodbye.
-Thank you.

51
00:04:08,102 --> 00:04:09,228
<i>Did you know?</i>

52
00:04:10,271 --> 00:04:12,106
<i>I even envy the telephone pole</i>

53
00:04:12,190 --> 00:04:13,941
<i>in front of your house</i>

54
00:04:14,609 --> 00:04:16,778
<i>and that smug little "We sell </i>gimbap<i>" sign</i>

55
00:04:16,861 --> 00:04:19,739
<i>hanging from your window bars.</i>

56
00:04:20,907 --> 00:04:24,702
<i>If only I could watch over you all day</i>
<i>like that pole,</i>

57
00:04:25,286 --> 00:04:30,041
<i>or hang from your window all day</i>
<i>like that sign…</i>

58
00:04:30,124 --> 00:04:31,542
WE SELL GIMBAP

59
00:04:32,251 --> 00:04:33,586
<i>I wish I could blow myself up</i>

60
00:04:34,295 --> 00:04:35,755
<i>like the big bang,</i>

61
00:04:35,838 --> 00:04:38,633
<i>so everything around you would be me.</i>

62
00:04:40,468 --> 00:04:41,302
<i>Especially,</i>

63
00:04:42,303 --> 00:04:43,846
<i>that damn </i>gimbap <i>lying on its back</i>

64
00:04:43,930 --> 00:04:46,557
<i>getting gently pressed and rolled</i>
<i>with your hands.</i>

65
00:04:48,059 --> 00:04:49,769
<i>If I could be that </i>gimbap…

66
00:04:50,978 --> 00:04:52,730
Eun-a!

67
00:04:57,443 --> 00:04:58,319
How cozy.

68
00:05:35,731 --> 00:05:40,778
You know, I could spend days imagining
punishing someone or plotting revenge.

69
00:05:41,362 --> 00:05:43,197
I get into the zone and stay up all night.

70
00:05:43,823 --> 00:05:44,907
I even forget to breathe.

71
00:05:45,783 --> 00:05:49,036
But happy imaginations don't last long
before they fall apart.

72
00:05:50,037 --> 00:05:50,955
Why is that?

73
00:05:52,206 --> 00:05:53,708
Is it because I haven't really been happy?

74
00:05:56,127 --> 00:05:58,880
When it falls apart, I get so anxious.

75
00:05:59,463 --> 00:06:01,674
I think I'll stay anxious
until I find success.

76
00:06:06,262 --> 00:06:07,847
<i>Is your brother at home?</i>

77
00:06:13,603 --> 00:06:15,563
<i>He went to Daegwallyeong</i>

78
00:06:15,646 --> 00:06:16,647
<i>to harvest napa cabbage.</i>

79
00:06:17,398 --> 00:06:19,567
<i>Napa cabbage. That's funny.</i>

80
00:06:21,527 --> 00:06:23,404
<i>When there's no welding work,</i>
<i>he does other things.</i>

81
00:06:24,488 --> 00:06:25,531
<i>That's good.</i>

82
00:06:27,783 --> 00:06:29,452
<i>Why are you suddenly asking about him?</i>

83
00:06:34,248 --> 00:06:36,876
<i>My grandmother made some braised kimchi,</i>

84
00:06:36,959 --> 00:06:38,252
<i>so I thought I'd drop some off.</i>

85
00:06:39,462 --> 00:06:40,880
<i>For both of you to enjoy.</i>

86
00:06:56,270 --> 00:06:57,605
<i>Then…</i>

87
00:06:57,688 --> 00:06:58,606
<i>Then…</i>

88
00:06:59,190 --> 00:07:00,942
<i>do you want to eat together?</i>

89
00:07:01,025 --> 00:07:02,610
<i>Do you want to eat together?</i>

90
00:07:04,445 --> 00:07:06,280
DO YOU WANT TO EAT TOGETHER?

91
00:07:07,907 --> 00:07:08,741
<i>Sounds great!</i>

92
00:07:10,701 --> 00:07:11,535
<i>Sounds good.</i>

93
00:07:58,124 --> 00:07:59,166
Careful, it's hot.

94
00:08:17,351 --> 00:08:18,686
Oh, come on!

95
00:08:22,732 --> 00:08:25,026
You said you were harvesting napa cabbage,
and you'd be gone all week!

96
00:08:25,610 --> 00:08:26,902
I said I was going next week.

97
00:08:28,821 --> 00:08:30,823
You've already been drinking?

98
00:08:31,866 --> 00:08:34,160
You could've at least cleaned the place.

99
00:08:34,243 --> 00:08:35,411
Just look at this mess.

100
00:08:43,711 --> 00:08:44,879
Hello.

101
00:08:47,923 --> 00:08:49,300
<i>Releasing steam.</i>

102
00:09:09,320 --> 00:09:10,571
Are those matching watches?

103
00:09:14,450 --> 00:09:16,118
Why are they flashing red?

104
00:09:18,079 --> 00:09:18,954
Jin-man.

105
00:09:19,622 --> 00:09:21,582
Don't just drink. Have some food too.

106
00:09:22,541 --> 00:09:24,627
Show some appreciation
for the person who brought it.

107
00:09:40,059 --> 00:09:41,143
What's your purpose in life?

108
00:09:46,774 --> 00:09:47,692
What about you?

109
00:09:47,775 --> 00:09:49,276
What's your purpose in life?

110
00:09:59,912 --> 00:10:01,789
What kind of person do you want to be?

111
00:10:02,373 --> 00:10:03,749
I'm not talking about being successful.

112
00:10:04,959 --> 00:10:07,253
A lot of successful people are idiots.

113
00:10:08,003 --> 00:10:09,755
"This is the kind of person I want to be."

114
00:10:10,798 --> 00:10:11,674
That kind of purpose.

115
00:10:14,427 --> 00:10:15,845
Just eat. It's okay.

116
00:10:22,143 --> 00:10:23,227
A strong mother.

117
00:10:27,898 --> 00:10:30,735
Yeah, I'm going to become a strong mother.

118
00:10:32,778 --> 00:10:35,364
Not the type of strength
that comes from money or connections.

119
00:10:37,408 --> 00:10:39,869
I want to become a mother
who remains calm in any situation.

120
00:10:42,705 --> 00:10:44,498
The kind who stands steady at the center

121
00:10:45,291 --> 00:10:46,208
so that

122
00:10:47,293 --> 00:10:50,337
her presence puts everyone
around her at ease.

123
00:10:51,046 --> 00:10:52,548
That's the kind of woman I want to be.

124
00:10:58,971 --> 00:11:00,181
Like my grandmother.

125
00:11:16,238 --> 00:11:17,072
What's wrong?

126
00:11:18,073 --> 00:11:18,991
What is it?

127
00:11:23,037 --> 00:11:23,871
What is it?

128
00:11:36,717 --> 00:11:37,927
Name?

129
00:11:39,512 --> 00:11:40,721
Byeon Eun-a.

130
00:11:40,804 --> 00:11:41,639
Eun-a.

131
00:11:58,197 --> 00:11:59,323
Thank you.

132
00:12:04,036 --> 00:12:07,122
DAYS LIKE THIS, DAYS LIKE THAT
POEMS BY HWANG JIN-MAN

133
00:12:07,206 --> 00:12:09,708
TO MS. BYEON EUN-A
I HOPE YOU BECOME A STRONG MOTHER

134
00:12:13,712 --> 00:12:14,838
Thank you.

135
00:12:21,845 --> 00:12:23,347
I'm giving in on everything else!

136
00:12:23,430 --> 00:12:25,599
How much more do I have to compromise?
I won't do it.

137
00:12:27,393 --> 00:12:29,228
-So scrap it?
-Scrap it!

138
00:12:29,812 --> 00:12:33,190
There's no point working with assholes
who boss you around from the start!

139
00:12:33,274 --> 00:12:34,108
Just scrap it!

140
00:12:36,986 --> 00:12:39,196
-Am I really asking for too much?
-Jeez.

141
00:12:39,280 --> 00:12:41,991
I thought things were good
since it was scheduled for streaming.

142
00:12:42,741 --> 00:12:45,578
<i>National Stress Management Department</i>
has been confirmed, right?

143
00:12:46,328 --> 00:12:47,663
It's been confirmed, but…

144
00:12:47,746 --> 00:12:48,998
But what?

145
00:12:49,081 --> 00:12:52,876
They said eight episodes might be too much
for Director Park to write alone,

146
00:12:52,960 --> 00:12:54,378
so they want to bring in another writer.

147
00:12:57,923 --> 00:12:59,800
That was a huge mistake.

148
00:12:59,883 --> 00:13:03,220
They really shouldn't have messed
with his writing.

149
00:13:04,013 --> 00:13:06,307
What is this, a factory?
Two writers don't mean faster writing.

150
00:13:06,390 --> 00:13:09,435
How dare they tell the director
to work with another writer?

151
00:13:09,518 --> 00:13:10,811
They're way out of line.

152
00:13:10,894 --> 00:13:12,730
-Does money give them control over me?
-Yeah, it does.

153
00:13:13,314 --> 00:13:15,065
It's my idea and my screenplay.

154
00:13:15,149 --> 00:13:16,900
If they don't like it,
I can take it somewhere else.

155
00:13:16,984 --> 00:13:17,860
Where, exactly?

156
00:13:18,444 --> 00:13:20,946
Go ahead, see if anyone
will offer you tens of billions of won.

157
00:13:22,448 --> 00:13:24,783
They can't even ask for that
when they're putting up tens of billions?

158
00:13:24,867 --> 00:13:26,619
This is your first series.

159
00:13:26,702 --> 00:13:30,497
Is getting help from someone experienced
really something to lose your temper over?

160
00:13:30,581 --> 00:13:32,041
That's what I'm saying.

161
00:13:32,124 --> 00:13:34,793
Why do they think I need help?

162
00:13:34,877 --> 00:13:37,630
-It's because they think I'm lacking!
-Seriously?

163
00:13:38,297 --> 00:13:40,049
As if you've never… Oh gosh.

164
00:13:40,132 --> 00:13:41,592
Who do they think they are?
I'm the director.

165
00:13:43,844 --> 00:13:46,055
I'm writing the whole thing,
from start to finish!

166
00:13:46,138 --> 00:13:47,806
Only Park Gyeong-se is allowed.

167
00:13:49,224 --> 00:13:50,893
These so-called directors
are all the same.

168
00:13:51,518 --> 00:13:54,772
Every single one of them
is just full of insecurity.

169
00:13:54,855 --> 00:13:56,649
You're all desperate to make it big,

170
00:13:56,732 --> 00:14:00,402
but you have this pathetic idea
that accepting help makes you weak

171
00:14:00,486 --> 00:14:01,987
and ruins your perfect image.

172
00:14:04,657 --> 00:14:06,867
Meanwhile, I'm wondering
how I'll pay the staff this month

173
00:14:06,950 --> 00:14:08,202
or how I'll pay the rent.

174
00:14:08,285 --> 00:14:11,455
I'm desperately looking
for any bit of help I can get.

175
00:14:11,538 --> 00:14:15,000
I'd welcome any help I can get
with open arms.

176
00:14:15,084 --> 00:14:16,210
I'd kill for some help!

177
00:14:23,425 --> 00:14:24,343
Listen carefully.

178
00:14:26,929 --> 00:14:27,805
Gyeong-se.

179
00:14:28,847 --> 00:14:29,682
You're old.

180
00:14:32,476 --> 00:14:33,394
You're outdated.

181
00:14:41,986 --> 00:14:43,153
You've been outdated for a long time.

182
00:14:43,237 --> 00:14:46,782
How many of your films start strong
only to fall apart later?

183
00:14:47,783 --> 00:14:50,744
That's why people say
you only ever lick the watermelon.

184
00:14:53,455 --> 00:14:56,625
Covering the losses
of a two-hour film is one thing,

185
00:14:56,709 --> 00:14:58,752
but there's no way to do that
for a series.

186
00:14:59,336 --> 00:15:02,047
If this fails,
there won't be a next time for you.

187
00:15:02,548 --> 00:15:04,717
So take the help.

188
00:15:05,467 --> 00:15:06,468
Take it while you can.

189
00:15:07,219 --> 00:15:08,679
How could you say that to me?

190
00:15:11,557 --> 00:15:12,433
Do you…

191
00:15:14,977 --> 00:15:16,145
even love me?

192
00:15:18,313 --> 00:15:19,606
Twenty years together,

193
00:15:19,690 --> 00:15:23,819
and you're still using outdated lines,
asking me if I love you.

194
00:15:23,902 --> 00:15:25,029
And you still want to write alone?

195
00:15:26,447 --> 00:15:29,116
Saying I don't love you
doesn't make me feel guilty,

196
00:15:29,825 --> 00:15:33,328
and saying that I do
doesn't benefit me at all, okay?

197
00:15:34,830 --> 00:15:36,498
Think this through.

198
00:15:37,708 --> 00:15:41,587
Byeon Eun-a can revive dead scripts,
and now she's backing Hwang Dong-man.

199
00:15:42,588 --> 00:15:45,507
Either sit there as the director
who flopped with <i>Sister Vengeance</i>

200
00:15:45,591 --> 00:15:47,676
and watch Hwang Dong-man
debut with a bang,

201
00:15:48,177 --> 00:15:50,095
or hurry up and put out something new.

202
00:15:51,513 --> 00:15:52,347
That's it.

203
00:16:07,446 --> 00:16:09,198
I'm sorry. I didn't know Jin-man was home.

204
00:16:09,907 --> 00:16:10,783
I bet it was uncomfortable.

205
00:16:11,950 --> 00:16:12,951
It was nice.

206
00:16:14,203 --> 00:16:16,538
I didn't think I had a purpose in life.

207
00:16:17,706 --> 00:16:20,834
But when he asked me out of the blue,
the answer just came out.

208
00:16:24,671 --> 00:16:26,256
Sometimes when you speak,

209
00:16:27,174 --> 00:16:29,635
it feels like our surroundings
turn into a vacuum.

210
00:16:30,886 --> 00:16:33,180
Earlier, when you were talking about
wanting to be a strong mother,

211
00:16:33,263 --> 00:16:35,140
all of a sudden, it felt like…

212
00:16:36,975 --> 00:16:38,310
the three of us were in space.

213
00:16:39,561 --> 00:16:40,979
And eating in space.

214
00:16:43,273 --> 00:16:46,026
I pictured us eating in space,
and it's funny.

215
00:16:57,454 --> 00:16:59,623
It's not a disaster alert message.

216
00:16:59,706 --> 00:17:01,250
It's from the emotion watch company.

217
00:17:01,333 --> 00:17:03,460
"Participant number 4000,

218
00:17:03,544 --> 00:17:06,004
your update is scheduled for Friday."

219
00:17:06,088 --> 00:17:07,256
What?

220
00:17:07,339 --> 00:17:09,842
Number 4000 out of 4000 people.
You're the last one.

221
00:17:09,925 --> 00:17:11,385
I barely made it in.

222
00:17:11,468 --> 00:17:12,719
I'm number 38.

223
00:17:14,471 --> 00:17:15,639
That's a winning hand.

224
00:17:17,224 --> 00:17:18,433
You got in early.

225
00:17:19,434 --> 00:17:20,853
Successful people are always like this.

226
00:17:21,478 --> 00:17:22,437
They always play it safe.

227
00:17:23,021 --> 00:17:25,524
Really? I think number 4000
feels way luckier.

228
00:17:25,607 --> 00:17:27,860
My life is always on the edge.

229
00:17:32,573 --> 00:17:33,907
It feels like winter is coming.

230
00:17:34,533 --> 00:17:36,160
Not a fan of winter?

231
00:17:36,243 --> 00:17:37,619
I like wind, though.

232
00:17:38,245 --> 00:17:40,330
I like the wind regardless of the season.

233
00:17:45,377 --> 00:17:46,253
Yes?

234
00:17:48,714 --> 00:17:49,590
Hello?

235
00:17:50,966 --> 00:17:52,551
<i>Is that you, Si-on?</i>

236
00:18:03,645 --> 00:18:04,521
It's me,

237
00:18:05,731 --> 00:18:06,565
Mom.

238
00:18:27,085 --> 00:18:29,171
-It was a wrong number.
-I see.

239
00:18:46,855 --> 00:18:48,649
The leaves are all falling here,

240
00:18:48,732 --> 00:18:51,193
but isn't it strange that Gangwon-do
still has fresh green napa cabbage?

241
00:18:56,615 --> 00:18:57,449
It's strange.

242
00:19:06,583 --> 00:19:08,543
Which sounds better,
Byeon Si-on or Byeon Eun-a?

243
00:19:09,127 --> 00:19:10,629
Byeon Eun-a is a hundred times better.

244
00:19:10,712 --> 00:19:11,838
Byeon Si-on is over the top.

245
00:19:13,215 --> 00:19:14,216
Are you changing your name?

246
00:19:14,716 --> 00:19:15,717
Nope.

247
00:19:15,801 --> 00:19:17,844
Byeon Eun-a is way better. I mean it.

248
00:19:21,348 --> 00:19:22,266
A long time ago…

249
00:19:28,146 --> 00:19:31,233
Jin-man said you shouldn't
go over the top with people's names.

250
00:19:31,858 --> 00:19:33,360
So he named my niece "Yeong-sil,"

251
00:19:34,194 --> 00:19:35,737
hoping she'd live an easygoing life.

252
00:19:37,155 --> 00:19:39,199
But her last name is "Hwang."

253
00:19:39,283 --> 00:19:40,242
Hwang Yeong-sil.

254
00:19:40,867 --> 00:19:42,327
She's bound for a tough life.

255
00:19:44,204 --> 00:19:45,289
"Yeong-sil."

256
00:19:46,581 --> 00:19:47,749
That's nice.

257
00:19:50,794 --> 00:19:52,587
How old is your niece now?

258
00:19:55,090 --> 00:19:55,966
Probably…

259
00:19:57,384 --> 00:19:58,427
14 years old,

260
00:19:59,636 --> 00:20:00,762
I think.

261
00:20:09,855 --> 00:20:11,231
Oh, let's go.

262
00:20:16,153 --> 00:20:17,195
It's so cold!

263
00:20:24,161 --> 00:20:24,995
Have a good night.

264
00:20:26,621 --> 00:20:27,539
Right.

265
00:20:27,622 --> 00:20:28,832
Good night.

266
00:20:28,915 --> 00:20:29,750
Here you go.

267
00:20:54,316 --> 00:20:55,192
Don't be so smug.

268
00:21:09,331 --> 00:21:10,332
<i>It's Mom.</i>

269
00:21:11,375 --> 00:21:12,334
<i>Call me.</i>

270
00:21:25,722 --> 00:21:26,681
What's going on?

271
00:21:28,100 --> 00:21:30,018
Why are you already drunk
so early in the evening?

272
00:21:34,815 --> 00:21:35,732
Just getting home now?

273
00:21:44,950 --> 00:21:46,410
Hey, hey, hey!

274
00:21:49,955 --> 00:21:50,956
A perfect ten.

275
00:21:58,588 --> 00:21:59,965
So tell me.

276
00:22:00,048 --> 00:22:03,051
Has it been nice and peaceful here
while I was gone?

277
00:22:04,136 --> 00:22:05,387
I have bad news, though.

278
00:22:05,470 --> 00:22:06,513
The peace ends today.

279
00:22:11,393 --> 00:22:13,562
Okay, okay.

280
00:22:17,441 --> 00:22:18,483
I'll accept your apology.

281
00:22:25,282 --> 00:22:26,950
Hey, what's all this?

282
00:22:27,033 --> 00:22:29,870
Oh, how I've missed the taste of home!

283
00:22:33,582 --> 00:22:35,000
Over the past 20 years,

284
00:22:36,042 --> 00:22:39,462
The Eight Club has split up
and gotten back together countless times.

285
00:22:40,505 --> 00:22:42,966
Hey, sit down. You're being distracting.

286
00:22:44,426 --> 00:22:47,804
After dragging Hwang Dong-man back,
we got back together again.

287
00:22:47,888 --> 00:22:49,431
I don't know how many more times

288
00:22:50,098 --> 00:22:51,892
we'll split apart and reunite, but--

289
00:22:51,975 --> 00:22:52,851
Hey, you punk.

290
00:22:53,852 --> 00:22:55,478
We're here because of you,
but you came last.

291
00:22:56,313 --> 00:22:57,480
Sorry.

292
00:22:57,564 --> 00:22:58,440
Damn it.

293
00:22:58,523 --> 00:22:59,733
Hey, go sit over there.

294
00:22:59,816 --> 00:23:01,109
-Next to Gyeong-se.
-No.

295
00:23:02,194 --> 00:23:03,945
-Go.
-No! Why?

296
00:23:04,029 --> 00:23:05,322
Go. Sit next to him!

297
00:23:05,405 --> 00:23:06,489
-Come on!
-Right now.

298
00:23:06,573 --> 00:23:08,700
You'll notice him less
if he's sitting next to you.

299
00:23:08,783 --> 00:23:10,535
-Come on.
-Hurry up and move! Hurry!

300
00:23:11,453 --> 00:23:12,329
Jeez.

301
00:23:12,412 --> 00:23:14,080
Seriously?

302
00:23:14,915 --> 00:23:17,667
These two punks
need to stay out of each other's sight,

303
00:23:17,751 --> 00:23:19,044
or they'll start snarling again.

304
00:23:21,880 --> 00:23:22,964
Oh my.

305
00:23:23,715 --> 00:23:25,383
It's nice not having to see him.

306
00:23:25,467 --> 00:23:28,178
Jeez, is there a way
I don't have to hear his voice either?

307
00:23:28,261 --> 00:23:30,180
Hey, bring me some super glue

308
00:23:30,263 --> 00:23:31,890
so I can plug his ears.

309
00:23:32,849 --> 00:23:36,436
From now on, sit side by side like this
so you can't see each other. Got it?

310
00:23:37,270 --> 00:23:39,231
Who'd have thought
these two used to be best friends?

311
00:23:39,314 --> 00:23:40,941
We weren't best friends.

312
00:23:41,024 --> 00:23:43,652
You think we were best friends
just because we lived together?

313
00:23:43,735 --> 00:23:45,570
Hey. Ormy and Gasmy.

314
00:23:45,654 --> 00:23:46,738
-Hey!
-Come on!

315
00:23:48,031 --> 00:23:49,115
-Jeez.
-Darn it.

316
00:23:49,199 --> 00:23:52,619
You'd be up all night watching movies,
but hesitate to say which ones.

317
00:23:52,702 --> 00:23:56,831
You'd be up all night watching porn,
then come to school with bloodshot eyes.

318
00:23:56,915 --> 00:24:00,377
Hey, who could be better friends
than Ormy and Gasmy?

319
00:24:00,460 --> 00:24:03,588
You two even share a sacred word
as your nicknames.

320
00:24:04,214 --> 00:24:05,966
Anyway, I dare you to fight again.

321
00:24:06,049 --> 00:24:09,552
If you do, I'll dig up your past again.

322
00:24:09,636 --> 00:24:11,012
Got it, Pyramid and Scheme?

323
00:24:11,096 --> 00:24:12,097
-Hey!
-Come on!

324
00:24:12,180 --> 00:24:13,974
-This takes me back.
-They were locked in a room

325
00:24:14,057 --> 00:24:15,684
because of that pyramid scheme.

326
00:24:15,767 --> 00:24:19,604
-Hey, cut it out!
-Let's toast. Come on, let's toast.

327
00:24:19,688 --> 00:24:20,772
Hey!

328
00:24:20,855 --> 00:24:23,525
-Here's to Ormy and Gasmy's reunion!
-Come on!

329
00:24:23,608 --> 00:24:25,944
-Cheers!
-Cheers!

330
00:24:29,197 --> 00:24:32,075
All of you need to write
while you're still young.

331
00:24:33,201 --> 00:24:35,996
When you get old, you can't crap or write.

332
00:24:36,746 --> 00:24:38,164
It's true.

333
00:24:38,248 --> 00:24:40,083
The power it takes to push out crap

334
00:24:40,166 --> 00:24:42,002
is proportional
to the power of creativity.

335
00:24:42,085 --> 00:24:43,795
Back in the day, I thought,

336
00:24:45,463 --> 00:24:46,381
"All right."

337
00:24:47,215 --> 00:24:50,218
"I'll just tear my ass open
and push it out."

338
00:24:50,302 --> 00:24:52,595
If I put my mind to it,
I could force it to come out.

339
00:24:53,471 --> 00:24:56,099
But now, I can't even imagine doing that.

340
00:24:56,182 --> 00:24:57,183
It'll end badly.

341
00:24:57,809 --> 00:24:59,311
It's the same with writing.

342
00:24:59,394 --> 00:25:00,687
Even if I stay up three nights straight,

343
00:25:01,688 --> 00:25:02,522
nothing comes out.

344
00:25:04,941 --> 00:25:08,570
It won't come out
because you're forcing it.

345
00:25:08,653 --> 00:25:10,947
You need to start your engine.

346
00:25:11,031 --> 00:25:12,490
Then it'll come out on its own.

347
00:25:12,574 --> 00:25:14,367
It'll come out while you're sleeping,

348
00:25:14,451 --> 00:25:16,328
or while you're taking a crap.

349
00:25:16,411 --> 00:25:18,121
It'll just pour out nonstop.

350
00:25:18,747 --> 00:25:21,082
We all have an engine inside us.

351
00:25:22,083 --> 00:25:23,501
So how exactly

352
00:25:24,294 --> 00:25:25,503
do you start that engine?

353
00:25:26,629 --> 00:25:29,341
That's the thing. I have no idea.

354
00:25:29,424 --> 00:25:30,425
It just started.

355
00:25:31,593 --> 00:25:33,303
I didn't even know I had one.

356
00:25:33,928 --> 00:25:35,722
He's saying you need to be in love.

357
00:25:36,723 --> 00:25:39,100
No, that's not it. It's something else.

358
00:25:39,893 --> 00:25:41,311
Everyone's got it.

359
00:25:41,394 --> 00:25:43,188
Beethoven had it,

360
00:25:43,271 --> 00:25:44,731
and I have it too.

361
00:25:46,107 --> 00:25:49,986
I read a biography on Beethoven as a kid,
and there was something I didn't get.

362
00:25:50,570 --> 00:25:51,613
While he was composing,

363
00:25:51,696 --> 00:25:54,240
he asked his servant
to bring him some warm milk.

364
00:25:54,324 --> 00:25:56,368
But as he was about to drink it,
he found it was cold.

365
00:25:56,451 --> 00:25:57,869
He was furious and asked,

366
00:25:57,952 --> 00:26:01,164
"I asked for warm milk,
so why is it cold?"

367
00:26:01,247 --> 00:26:02,916
But it turns out,

368
00:26:02,999 --> 00:26:05,168
the servant brought him warm milk,

369
00:26:05,251 --> 00:26:06,753
but he was so focused on composing

370
00:26:06,836 --> 00:26:08,963
that he didn't notice
the milk had gone cold.

371
00:26:09,964 --> 00:26:10,924
That made me wonder,

372
00:26:11,424 --> 00:26:14,302
"How could you not notice something
right next to you?"

373
00:26:14,386 --> 00:26:15,595
But guess what?

374
00:26:16,221 --> 00:26:20,558
I was writing like crazy yesterday
and thought,

375
00:26:20,642 --> 00:26:22,268
"I should get some coffee."

376
00:26:22,352 --> 00:26:23,645
<i>So I got up, but then…</i>

377
00:26:26,731 --> 00:26:27,982
<i>it was already brewed.</i>

378
00:26:28,691 --> 00:26:29,901
<i>And it was cold.</i>

379
00:26:30,735 --> 00:26:32,070
<i>I forgot I had brewed it.</i>

380
00:26:33,571 --> 00:26:35,865
I felt a sense of pride,
like I was on the same level

381
00:26:36,616 --> 00:26:37,700
as Beethoven.

382
00:26:37,784 --> 00:26:38,993
You forgot because it was coffee.

383
00:26:39,077 --> 00:26:40,578
You wouldn't have
if it had been instant noodles.

384
00:26:41,162 --> 00:26:42,539
Goddamn it.

385
00:26:43,039 --> 00:26:44,707
Are you trying to ruin the mood, sir?

386
00:26:44,791 --> 00:26:46,709
You know why we started
hating each other so much?

387
00:26:47,961 --> 00:26:50,547
You could say
it's like hating your own kind.

388
00:26:51,131 --> 00:26:52,006
Both of us

389
00:26:52,924 --> 00:26:53,758
are frivolous.

390
00:26:53,842 --> 00:26:56,428
We're too shallow and can't dive deep.

391
00:26:56,511 --> 00:26:58,555
That's why our writing is full of seaweed,

392
00:26:58,638 --> 00:27:00,557
and not a single abalone or conch.

393
00:27:01,808 --> 00:27:04,602
But now, I'm able to dive deep.

394
00:27:05,186 --> 00:27:07,063
I can even get abalone,

395
00:27:07,147 --> 00:27:10,400
which means we're no longer the same kind.

396
00:27:12,569 --> 00:27:13,570
Bye.

397
00:27:22,412 --> 00:27:23,830
Have some watermelon,

398
00:27:23,913 --> 00:27:26,207
instead of always licking the surface.

399
00:27:26,291 --> 00:27:28,918
You've got to taste the sweetness.

400
00:27:37,969 --> 00:27:39,471
Brace for impact.

401
00:27:39,554 --> 00:27:41,014
Brace for impact.

402
00:27:51,524 --> 00:27:52,525
Just watch.

403
00:27:53,276 --> 00:27:54,777
I'm about to blow up.

404
00:28:03,745 --> 00:28:04,621
It was a good read.

405
00:28:06,623 --> 00:28:10,627
There are so many good lines
you can't help but stop and take them in.

406
00:28:10,710 --> 00:28:13,463
Read it, pick ten expressions you like,
and keep them in mind.

407
00:28:14,172 --> 00:28:15,632
Then at some point,

408
00:28:15,715 --> 00:28:17,675
the right words will come out
at the right time.

409
00:28:19,385 --> 00:28:21,888
Then people will look at you differently.

410
00:28:24,015 --> 00:28:25,892
Did you finish the book
I gave you last time?

411
00:28:27,268 --> 00:28:28,686
Stop looking at your phone.

412
00:28:30,522 --> 00:28:32,023
Another person came forward.

413
00:28:32,106 --> 00:28:34,192
They claim to be
your biological daughter's classmate.

414
00:28:37,987 --> 00:28:39,948
There's even a photo
from when she was young.

415
00:28:51,459 --> 00:28:53,670
THAT SLOPPY GIMBAP
SHE CLEARLY MADE HERSELF…

416
00:28:53,753 --> 00:28:56,214
I STILL THINK ABOUT THAT SAD GIMBAP
AND HOW SHE FORCED IT DOWN

417
00:29:16,985 --> 00:29:19,737
I don't think
we can stay silent any longer.

418
00:29:20,321 --> 00:29:23,449
We should do an exclusive interview
and get an article out today.

419
00:29:23,533 --> 00:29:26,369
Reporter Yeom's on standby
and said to call anytime.

420
00:29:26,452 --> 00:29:29,914
After you return from today's event,
the office will be ready for an interview.

421
00:29:29,998 --> 00:29:31,249
Forget it.

422
00:29:31,332 --> 00:29:33,042
I'll tell them myself.

423
00:29:33,126 --> 00:29:35,003
On live TV, in front of the whole country.

424
00:29:35,086 --> 00:29:37,881
But doing nothing until the award ceremony
won't sit well with the public.

425
00:29:37,964 --> 00:29:40,341
People with nothing better to do

426
00:29:40,425 --> 00:29:43,386
get their entertainment
from watching celebrities crash and burn.

427
00:29:43,469 --> 00:29:44,721
Let them enjoy it for a few more days.

428
00:29:48,558 --> 00:29:50,685
-Thank you for today.
-All right, bye.

429
00:29:53,855 --> 00:29:54,939
Mr. Hwang.

430
00:29:56,065 --> 00:29:57,442
Would you like to grab a bite?

431
00:29:57,525 --> 00:29:59,193
I have to go home and make revisions.

432
00:29:59,277 --> 00:30:00,612
Even an all-nighter won't cut it

433
00:30:00,695 --> 00:30:02,196
if I want to start casting next week.

434
00:30:03,448 --> 00:30:05,909
I finally get to show my screenplay
to actors.

435
00:30:06,701 --> 00:30:09,537
Mr. Hwang, promise you'll invite me
to the premiere.

436
00:30:10,997 --> 00:30:12,707
Bring both your parents.

437
00:30:13,291 --> 00:30:15,501
I'll seat you right in the center,
right next to the cast.

438
00:30:15,585 --> 00:30:16,586
Thank you.

439
00:30:16,669 --> 00:30:17,754
-Let's go.
-Okay.

440
00:30:29,307 --> 00:30:30,141
Mr. Hwang.

441
00:30:32,518 --> 00:30:33,561
You've got this.

442
00:30:34,312 --> 00:30:35,480
You've got this, Myeong-hun!

443
00:30:36,898 --> 00:30:38,107
-See you.
-Goodbye.

444
00:31:11,391 --> 00:31:12,225
Hey.

445
00:31:14,811 --> 00:31:16,771
<i>Days like this are worth living</i>

446
00:31:18,648 --> 00:31:19,732
<i>If you've gone far away</i>

447
00:31:20,608 --> 00:31:21,985
<i>It would be a good day to come back</i>

448
00:32:11,409 --> 00:32:12,326
Jin-man…

449
00:34:02,520 --> 00:34:03,896
CATERING COMPANY OWNER

450
00:34:03,980 --> 00:34:05,148
<i>I'm sorry.</i>

451
00:34:05,231 --> 00:34:08,985
<i>Something came up at home,</i>
<i>so I can't come to work today.</i>

452
00:34:40,183 --> 00:34:41,017
Where are you going?

453
00:34:43,603 --> 00:34:45,021
Where else? I'm going to work.

454
00:36:13,359 --> 00:36:14,402
-Are they here?
-Yes, sir.

455
00:36:25,329 --> 00:36:26,622
Oh Jeong-hui deserves

456
00:36:27,581 --> 00:36:28,958
whatever shit I want to give her.

457
00:36:29,750 --> 00:36:30,668
That's where she stands.

458
00:36:30,751 --> 00:36:33,671
She can't say a word,
no matter what I say. Why?

459
00:36:33,754 --> 00:36:36,549
She tarnished a blockbuster movie
when her family scandal broke out

460
00:36:36,632 --> 00:36:40,136
and halted post-production
on another finished movie.

461
00:36:40,219 --> 00:36:41,595
But did you know

462
00:36:43,055 --> 00:36:45,099
that Oh Jeong-hui gave me shit instead?

463
00:36:45,808 --> 00:36:47,226
She called first thing this morning

464
00:36:48,144 --> 00:36:52,189
bitching about how I gave her daughter
a crappy screenplay like this.

465
00:36:52,815 --> 00:36:54,108
This is exactly why

466
00:36:55,192 --> 00:36:57,445
I told you not to give screenplays
directly to the actor.

467
00:36:58,279 --> 00:37:02,283
How the hell can the CEO not know
which actor got the screenplay?

468
00:37:04,243 --> 00:37:05,202
I'm sorry, sir.

469
00:37:05,828 --> 00:37:07,913
Get ready to be chewed out by an actor.

470
00:37:29,894 --> 00:37:30,853
It's Jang Mi-ran!

471
00:37:31,437 --> 00:37:32,521
-It's Jang Mi-ran!
-Jang Mi-ran?

472
00:37:32,605 --> 00:37:34,482
-Wow.
-It's Jang Mi-ran.

473
00:37:37,109 --> 00:37:40,029
<i>Right now, this feels like a dream</i>

474
00:37:40,112 --> 00:37:43,991
<i>You and me, dancing together</i>

475
00:37:44,075 --> 00:37:46,994
<i>The feeling is so incredible</i>
<i>It's like art</i>

476
00:37:47,078 --> 00:37:49,538
-Jang Mi-ran!
-Jang Mi-ran!

477
00:37:49,622 --> 00:37:52,792
-Jang Mi-ran!
-Jang Mi-ran!

478
00:37:54,960 --> 00:37:56,629
This way please, Mi-ran.

479
00:37:58,589 --> 00:37:59,715
You're so pretty!

480
00:37:59,799 --> 00:38:01,300
She's seriously pretty.

481
00:38:04,595 --> 00:38:06,806
-Hello.
-Gosh, it's hot.

482
00:38:11,394 --> 00:38:12,686
Can you believe it?

483
00:38:13,854 --> 00:38:16,857
Only 310,000 people watched the movie
I worked my ass off for,

484
00:38:17,358 --> 00:38:19,860
but a video of me half-ass singing
at a wedding got five million views.

485
00:38:19,944 --> 00:38:21,153
Maybe I should quit acting.

486
00:38:22,196 --> 00:38:23,239
Who's this?

487
00:38:23,322 --> 00:38:25,116
This is Producer Byeon Eun-a
from Choi Film.

488
00:38:25,199 --> 00:38:26,075
She's working with me.

489
00:38:27,201 --> 00:38:28,244
Nice to meet you.

490
00:38:30,454 --> 00:38:32,957
With a face like that,
you should be an actor, not a producer.

491
00:38:34,500 --> 00:38:36,001
Never mind. Don't be an actor.

492
00:38:37,169 --> 00:38:38,879
When I was a sophomore in high school,

493
00:38:38,963 --> 00:38:43,342
I went through hell
because I couldn't eat or sleep.

494
00:38:43,426 --> 00:38:46,512
I went to a bunch of hospitals, but no one
could figure out what was wrong.

495
00:38:47,138 --> 00:38:51,225
Then my grandma went to a shaman,
who told her I was chosen by spirits

496
00:38:51,308 --> 00:38:52,560
and destined to be a shaman.

497
00:38:53,310 --> 00:38:56,647
They say a shaman's fate
and an actor's fate are similar,

498
00:38:56,730 --> 00:38:59,442
so I had to become an actor
if I didn't want to become a shaman.

499
00:39:01,277 --> 00:39:02,611
I should've just become a shaman.

500
00:39:03,320 --> 00:39:04,613
Acting is more exhausting.

501
00:39:04,697 --> 00:39:06,157
At least shamans don't saw off their arms.

502
00:39:07,616 --> 00:39:09,535
I saw off my arm once,
and now every role is the same.

503
00:39:09,618 --> 00:39:12,913
They hack away with an ax
or a pair of scissors.

504
00:39:13,497 --> 00:39:14,665
Director Lee Jun-hwan,

505
00:39:15,749 --> 00:39:18,085
I liked you because you looked polished.

506
00:39:18,752 --> 00:39:20,880
So how is it that your writing
is so crappy?

507
00:39:25,259 --> 00:39:26,427
A SCARY WOMAN

508
00:39:26,510 --> 00:39:30,055
You can't use actors like this, all right?

509
00:39:30,139 --> 00:39:31,724
Directors need to reflect on this.

510
00:39:38,606 --> 00:39:40,316
It doesn't matter
if you saw off an arm or a leg.

511
00:39:42,693 --> 00:39:46,739
Do you know the biggest flaw
in the characters you've been playing?

512
00:39:47,990 --> 00:39:49,617
It's that they know
how to use their heads.

513
00:39:52,495 --> 00:39:53,871
But you don't know how to use yours.

514
00:39:55,331 --> 00:39:56,916
Every time I watch you in a movie

515
00:39:56,999 --> 00:40:01,545
and see you act sophisticated,
I lose it because it doesn't suit you.

516
00:40:02,129 --> 00:40:04,798
Why does someone
with such raw, natural appeal in real life

517
00:40:05,549 --> 00:40:08,844
start to use her head
the second she's on camera?

518
00:40:09,845 --> 00:40:14,391
We want a larger-than-life Jang Mi-ran
who can make us burst out laughing.

519
00:40:15,309 --> 00:40:17,811
We don't want to watch someone
who's just like us,

520
00:40:17,895 --> 00:40:20,231
struggling to use their head.

521
00:40:25,569 --> 00:40:26,445
A SCARY WOMAN

522
00:40:26,529 --> 00:40:27,947
This character doesn't use her head,

523
00:40:28,906 --> 00:40:30,449
so she doesn't think things through

524
00:40:30,533 --> 00:40:31,867
or make calculations.

525
00:40:32,910 --> 00:40:33,744
She's someone…

526
00:40:35,496 --> 00:40:37,331
who's driven purely by her heart.

527
00:40:39,833 --> 00:40:41,126
That's what makes her scary.

528
00:40:43,879 --> 00:40:47,299
Isn't this the first role
that fits you perfectly?

529
00:40:55,391 --> 00:40:56,308
Hey.

530
00:41:20,666 --> 00:41:22,042
That bitch is Ax, right?

531
00:41:22,126 --> 00:41:23,586
The one who got screwed
for running her mouth?

532
00:41:31,969 --> 00:41:33,345
Put that crazy bitch on the phone.

533
00:41:34,013 --> 00:41:35,389
Just put her on!

534
00:41:44,189 --> 00:41:45,024
Yes?

535
00:41:45,107 --> 00:41:46,108
Hey.

536
00:41:46,900 --> 00:41:48,319
Answer my calls from now on.

537
00:41:48,944 --> 00:41:51,405
The second you start kissing my ass,
you're done.

538
00:41:58,162 --> 00:41:59,288
What did she say?

539
00:41:59,872 --> 00:42:01,081
I think she'll do it.

540
00:42:21,101 --> 00:42:22,102
Excuse me.

541
00:42:24,063 --> 00:42:26,523
Please answer Ms. Oh Jeong-hui's call.

542
00:42:28,400 --> 00:42:29,234
I really hope

543
00:42:30,569 --> 00:42:31,654
you answer her call today.

544
00:42:34,031 --> 00:42:35,032
Please.

545
00:43:30,087 --> 00:43:31,964
HANKUK UNIVERSITY GRADUATE SCHOOL
KOREAN LITERATURE, HWANG JIN-MAN

546
00:44:12,296 --> 00:44:14,423
BIRTH REGISTRATION
NAME: HWANG YEONG-SIL

547
00:44:32,232 --> 00:44:34,943
NATIONAL CENTER
FOR THE RIGHTS OF THE CHILD

548
00:44:42,618 --> 00:44:43,702
I'm sorry.

549
00:44:44,411 --> 00:44:46,580
We're legally prohibited
from disclosing information

550
00:44:46,663 --> 00:44:48,415
about adoptive parents.

551
00:44:49,458 --> 00:44:50,375
At this point,

552
00:44:50,459 --> 00:44:53,545
your only option is to wait for your niece
to look for her biological parents.

553
00:44:54,713 --> 00:44:57,758
How could she have been adopted
without her father's knowledge?

554
00:44:57,841 --> 00:44:59,218
Even if they were divorced,

555
00:45:00,010 --> 00:45:02,054
how can you do that
without the father's consent?

556
00:45:02,638 --> 00:45:06,308
Records show that the father's whereabouts
were unknown at the time,

557
00:45:06,934 --> 00:45:09,603
so the mother's consent alone
was sufficient.

558
00:45:12,189 --> 00:45:13,774
Can I get her address?

559
00:45:14,650 --> 00:45:16,151
Can't you at least tell me that?

560
00:45:18,904 --> 00:45:21,156
It's too harsh not to know

561
00:45:22,866 --> 00:45:24,034
where your child lives.

562
00:45:31,959 --> 00:45:33,794
<i>I'm not even hoping for success.</i>

563
00:45:34,711 --> 00:45:35,546
<i>I just</i>

564
00:45:36,421 --> 00:45:38,257
<i>don't want to be miserable.</i>

565
00:46:16,420 --> 00:46:17,337
What is it?

566
00:46:19,089 --> 00:46:20,382
Did your engine die?

567
00:46:25,262 --> 00:46:27,055
Is it better that we all succeed together,

568
00:46:28,765 --> 00:46:30,142
or that we all go down together?

569
00:46:30,851 --> 00:46:32,936
It's easier if we all go down together.

570
00:46:35,939 --> 00:46:38,150
Going down isn't so bad.

571
00:46:39,318 --> 00:46:40,193
As long as we're together.

572
00:46:46,992 --> 00:46:48,535
It's funny, you know.

573
00:46:50,370 --> 00:46:52,539
When I'm deep in my feelings…

574
00:46:54,458 --> 00:46:55,542
I don't hate you.

575
00:46:57,753 --> 00:46:59,838
I don't hate you when I'm really happy.

576
00:47:01,923 --> 00:47:02,758
And I don't hate you

577
00:47:03,842 --> 00:47:05,177
when I feel really sad.

578
00:47:42,506 --> 00:47:44,716
FIVE HUNDRED WON

579
00:48:14,788 --> 00:48:15,914
Yes?

580
00:48:18,208 --> 00:48:19,251
Is that you, Si-on?

581
00:48:20,544 --> 00:48:21,586
This is Eun-a.

582
00:48:22,337 --> 00:48:23,338
Byeon Eun-a.

583
00:48:24,589 --> 00:48:25,924
Why did you change your name?

584
00:48:30,971 --> 00:48:32,806
I didn't know your father had died.

585
00:48:33,682 --> 00:48:36,059
<i>Don't worry anymore. You've got me now.</i>

586
00:48:38,520 --> 00:48:40,731
Now you can do

587
00:48:40,814 --> 00:48:42,065
whatever you want.

588
00:48:42,899 --> 00:48:44,359
If you want,

589
00:48:44,943 --> 00:48:46,820
<i>you can start studying again.</i>

590
00:48:48,238 --> 00:48:49,656
<i>You can even study abroad.</i>

591
00:48:50,699 --> 00:48:52,701
<i>Tell me if there's anywhere</i>
<i>you want to go.</i>

592
00:48:53,326 --> 00:48:54,703
<i>I'll make it happen.</i>

593
00:48:54,786 --> 00:48:55,620
Why?

594
00:48:56,913 --> 00:48:58,415
Are you still ashamed of me?

595
00:48:59,916 --> 00:49:01,918
<i>Do you want me to disappear</i>
<i>somewhere far away?</i>

596
00:49:02,002 --> 00:49:03,003
Or are you

597
00:49:03,754 --> 00:49:04,921
trying to save yourself

598
00:49:05,756 --> 00:49:07,841
by taking back the daughter you abandoned?

599
00:49:07,924 --> 00:49:09,301
I never abandoned you.

600
00:49:09,384 --> 00:49:10,802
It was your father I left.

601
00:49:11,636 --> 00:49:14,681
If I had known he died,
I would've come for you right away.

602
00:49:15,348 --> 00:49:17,017
Because you're no longer
Byeon Jae-su's daughter,

603
00:49:17,601 --> 00:49:18,810
but Oh Jeong-hui's daughter.

604
00:49:19,394 --> 00:49:21,188
I know that you're desperate,

605
00:49:22,063 --> 00:49:24,608
but I have my own memories,
and you have yours.

606
00:49:25,567 --> 00:49:27,277
Don't pretend you're a caring mother.

607
00:49:28,862 --> 00:49:30,280
<i>A nine-year-old kid wanted to hide</i>

608
00:49:30,864 --> 00:49:32,157
the fact that she'd been abandoned,

609
00:49:32,240 --> 00:49:34,409
so she made her own <i>gimbap</i>
for the field trip.

610
00:49:35,076 --> 00:49:37,204
I didn't want anyone to know
that the poor kid was me,

611
00:49:37,287 --> 00:49:39,790
so I even changed my name
and lived a quiet life.

612
00:49:40,749 --> 00:49:41,708
<i>What I'm saying is</i>

613
00:49:42,709 --> 00:49:45,253
<i>don't let anyone find out</i>
<i>that the poor kid Oh Jeong-hui abandoned</i>

614
00:49:46,630 --> 00:49:48,006
is actually me.

615
00:49:50,550 --> 00:49:52,135
I won't let anyone find out either.

616
00:50:09,861 --> 00:50:10,862
We're here, ma'am.

617
00:50:31,049 --> 00:50:33,969
46TH KOREAN FILM AWARDS

618
00:50:44,813 --> 00:50:45,647
<i>Go ahead and leave!</i>

619
00:50:45,730 --> 00:50:48,233
All you've done is leech off my name!

620
00:50:57,576 --> 00:50:58,535
Byeon Si-on.

621
00:51:00,328 --> 00:51:01,621
Byeon Si-on!

622
00:51:15,051 --> 00:51:18,138
The 46th Korean Film Awards
Best Actress Award goes to…

623
00:51:22,726 --> 00:51:23,894
MY MOTHER, OH JEONG-HUI
AUTUMN SCENES, KANG JI-EUN

624
00:51:23,977 --> 00:51:26,187
Oh Jeong-hui from <i>My Mother</i>.

625
00:51:26,271 --> 00:51:27,522
Congratulations!

626
00:51:37,324 --> 00:51:39,117
BEST ACTRESS AWARD
MY MOTHER, OH JEONG-HUI

627
00:51:52,797 --> 00:51:53,757
Thank you.

628
00:51:57,010 --> 00:52:00,722
They say actors are happy to be on stage,
even if they're just a tree.

629
00:52:01,348 --> 00:52:03,850
I would've been happy just being a tree,

630
00:52:03,934 --> 00:52:07,228
so I find it amazing
that I've gotten this far.

631
00:52:07,312 --> 00:52:10,732
At the same time, another part of me
feels it was inevitable,

632
00:52:10,815 --> 00:52:14,527
as if I've been walking a path
that was already decided.

633
00:52:15,737 --> 00:52:18,531
I'm feeling both emotions so distinctly,

634
00:52:19,157 --> 00:52:21,117
like my heart is split in two.

635
00:52:21,868 --> 00:52:24,829
"This is amazing. How did I get this far?"

636
00:52:24,913 --> 00:52:26,665
"But wasn't it inevitable?"

637
00:52:28,041 --> 00:52:30,502
I also feel joy and fear at the same time.

638
00:52:31,920 --> 00:52:34,714
The joy of achievement.

639
00:52:35,799 --> 00:52:40,303
And the fear that this crown was gained
at the expense of others.

640
00:52:40,887 --> 00:52:43,098
It feels like this came
at the expense of the crew's hard work.

641
00:52:45,016 --> 00:52:46,101
And it feels like…

642
00:52:48,728 --> 00:52:51,147
this came at the expense of the happiness

643
00:52:52,857 --> 00:52:55,151
of my young daughter,
whom I couldn't be there for.

644
00:52:57,612 --> 00:52:59,114
<i>That guilt</i>

645
00:53:00,115 --> 00:53:01,950
led to my regret
over not being able to love Mi-ran

646
00:53:03,618 --> 00:53:05,286
as fully as I wanted.

647
00:53:08,748 --> 00:53:11,292
I approached <i>My Mother</i>

648
00:53:11,376 --> 00:53:15,922
in the hopes of bringing comfort
to mothers like me who carry many regrets,

649
00:53:16,506 --> 00:53:18,591
and to the daughters

650
00:53:18,675 --> 00:53:20,552
who have had to endure imperfect mothers.

651
00:53:22,345 --> 00:53:23,638
I dedicate this great award

652
00:53:24,264 --> 00:53:26,391
to mothers and daughters everywhere

653
00:53:26,474 --> 00:53:29,060
who have gone through difficult times

654
00:53:30,228 --> 00:53:32,731
and who have continued on despite them.

655
00:54:28,119 --> 00:54:29,120
Are you okay?

656
00:54:29,746 --> 00:54:31,831
Let me help you get down. Slowly.

657
00:54:37,462 --> 00:54:39,380
If you could erase your childhood memories

658
00:54:39,464 --> 00:54:41,382
to escape from this pain,

659
00:54:43,176 --> 00:54:44,427
would you do it?

660
00:54:48,848 --> 00:54:49,766
No.

661
00:54:51,810 --> 00:54:54,270
<i>Without this pain, that woman…</i>

662
00:54:57,524 --> 00:54:59,359
<i>would be completely innocent.</i>

663
00:55:29,139 --> 00:55:31,349
I've never gotten an "unknown."

664
00:55:32,392 --> 00:55:34,435
You might not have seen it,

665
00:55:34,519 --> 00:55:35,895
but it showed up twice.

666
00:55:37,230 --> 00:55:39,232
It first appeared on October 29th.

667
00:55:42,110 --> 00:55:43,486
I don't remember it at all.

668
00:55:44,112 --> 00:55:45,655
When "unknown" appears,

669
00:55:45,738 --> 00:55:48,199
one or two minutes
of the situation are recorded

670
00:55:48,283 --> 00:55:51,703
because participants
might not recall the situation.

671
00:55:52,620 --> 00:55:53,955
Would you like to listen to it?

672
00:55:55,540 --> 00:55:59,294
RECORDING

673
00:56:01,421 --> 00:56:02,255
<i>Jin-man.</i>

674
00:56:05,133 --> 00:56:06,426
<i>What are you doing?</i>

675
00:56:07,510 --> 00:56:08,928
<i>Weren't you drinking?</i>

676
00:56:10,722 --> 00:56:11,723
Come down.

677
00:56:14,517 --> 00:56:15,768
It's okay. Come down.

678
00:56:19,439 --> 00:56:20,857
You need to eat something.

679
00:56:22,609 --> 00:56:24,819
<i>I'll make kimchi fried rice, so come down.</i>

680
00:56:31,451 --> 00:56:34,621
And it appeared again recently.

681
00:56:35,246 --> 00:56:36,456
Let's listen to it.

682
00:56:38,917 --> 00:56:41,669
RECORDING

683
00:57:07,528 --> 00:57:08,655
I'm sorry.

684
00:57:11,908 --> 00:57:14,035
What kind of emotion do you think this is?

685
00:57:16,829 --> 00:57:18,248
I'm not sure.

686
00:57:20,416 --> 00:57:22,460
What should I call this?

687
00:57:27,257 --> 00:57:30,677
There's someone else who keeps showing
the same emotion pattern.

688
00:57:33,805 --> 00:57:35,598
Please send me the data for number 38.

689
00:57:36,432 --> 00:57:37,684
I'm number 38.

690
00:57:38,393 --> 00:57:39,394
That's a winning hand.

691
00:57:42,230 --> 00:57:44,190
Let's see, number 38…

692
00:57:46,192 --> 00:57:48,403
This person has trauma
from being abandoned as a child,

693
00:57:48,486 --> 00:57:51,948
so when they encounter similar situations,
they feel this emotion.

694
00:57:52,574 --> 00:57:53,741
They experience it often.

695
00:57:53,825 --> 00:57:55,285
About once or twice a month?

696
00:57:57,829 --> 00:57:58,913
This person

697
00:57:59,539 --> 00:58:02,875
described the feeling
as "self-destructive."

698
00:58:03,835 --> 00:58:05,253
<i>"Self-destructive."</i>

699
00:58:12,468 --> 00:58:13,595
What about you?

700
00:58:14,178 --> 00:58:16,097
<i>How would you describe it?</i>

701
00:58:17,974 --> 00:58:20,685
<i>What kind of emotion would you call this?</i>

702
00:58:23,980 --> 00:58:26,608
<i>Does "self-destructive" feel right?</i>

703
00:58:29,235 --> 00:58:33,239
It's a little different
from typical "anger" or "frustration"

704
00:58:33,865 --> 00:58:37,243
because there's about 7%
of some kind of desperation mixed in.

705
00:58:41,831 --> 00:58:43,416
I can see this is getting hard for you.

706
00:58:43,499 --> 00:58:44,792
Should we continue later?

707
00:58:44,876 --> 00:58:47,128
You can tell me about it later.

708
00:58:55,094 --> 00:58:55,970
"Help me."

709
00:59:00,224 --> 00:59:01,517
It's a cry for help.

710
00:59:05,897 --> 00:59:07,315
The 7% of desperation…

711
00:59:09,817 --> 00:59:10,943
is "help me."

712
00:59:13,363 --> 00:59:14,280
<i>Help me.</i>

713
00:59:16,282 --> 00:59:17,200
<i>Words…</i>

714
00:59:19,243 --> 00:59:20,995
<i>I've never spoken in my life.</i>

715
00:59:24,123 --> 00:59:25,041
<i>Help me.</i>

716
00:59:41,140 --> 00:59:45,978
Do you remember when I said someone else
shared the same pattern as you?

717
00:59:47,397 --> 00:59:48,272
Number 4000.

718
00:59:50,108 --> 00:59:51,818
"Participant number 4000."

719
00:59:51,901 --> 00:59:53,069
What?

720
00:59:53,152 --> 00:59:54,487
Number 4000 out of 4000 people.

721
00:59:54,570 --> 00:59:55,613
<i>You're the last one.</i>

722
00:59:56,614 --> 00:59:59,117
They showed the same pattern as you again.

723
01:00:00,326 --> 01:00:03,496
It's the same pattern that you exhibit
when you get nosebleeds,

724
01:00:03,996 --> 01:00:06,541
and this is how they described
the emotion.

725
01:00:08,543 --> 01:00:09,502
"Help me."

726
01:00:12,213 --> 01:00:13,506
They said

727
01:00:14,340 --> 01:00:16,759
it was like a desperate cry for help.

728
01:00:18,010 --> 01:00:18,970
Do you agree?

729
01:00:42,076 --> 01:00:43,202
Why does…

730
01:00:45,079 --> 01:00:46,998
Why does that person feel that way?

731
01:03:20,985 --> 01:03:21,819
I'll help you.

732
01:03:29,911 --> 01:03:32,330
We're already helping each other

733
01:03:34,123 --> 01:03:34,957
a lot.

734
01:03:46,594 --> 01:03:47,678
We're in sync.

735
01:03:56,103 --> 01:03:57,021
In sync.

736
01:04:38,562 --> 01:04:39,397
Let's…

737
01:04:45,486 --> 01:04:46,404
find Yeong-sil.

738
01:05:34,535 --> 01:05:36,746
<i>What kind of weather</i>
<i>should I make for you?</i>

739
01:05:38,748 --> 01:05:40,666
<i>Tell me what kind of weather</i>
<i>you like, Eun-a,</i>

740
01:05:41,375 --> 01:05:42,543
<i>and I'll make it for you.</i>

741
01:05:45,421 --> 01:05:46,255
I like

742
01:05:46,922 --> 01:05:48,382
any kind of wind.

743
01:07:09,880 --> 01:07:11,257
<i>A happy imagination…</i>

744
01:07:13,008 --> 01:07:13,884
<i>complete.</i>

745
01:07:36,574 --> 01:07:38,242
WE ARE ALL TRYING HERE

746
01:07:56,844 --> 01:07:58,387
<i>You know exactly where I'm stuck</i>

747
01:07:58,971 --> 01:08:02,391
<i>and where I need to focus emotionally</i>
<i>so I can really take off.</i>

748
01:08:02,975 --> 01:08:04,268
<i>Is it okay to use "Yeong-sil"</i>

749
01:08:04,351 --> 01:08:06,228
<i>as my pseudonym?</i>

750
01:08:06,312 --> 01:08:08,772
I told you I'm not co-writing,
so why'd you set up an appointment?

751
01:08:09,440 --> 01:08:12,151
<i>You're the funniest director</i>
<i>I've ever met.</i>

752
01:08:13,444 --> 01:08:15,237
<i>No one our age has this kind of stamina.</i>

753
01:08:15,321 --> 01:08:17,698
-I never knew work could be so much fun.
<i>-Who likes who exactly?</i>

754
01:08:18,699 --> 01:08:20,409
<i>Whenever I feel like I'm being abandoned,</i>

755
01:08:20,492 --> 01:08:22,411
<i>my whole body aches, and I get nosebleeds.</i>

756
01:08:23,204 --> 01:08:25,247
<i>The first person who wrote me off.</i>

757
01:08:25,748 --> 01:08:26,624
